# Coach speech (Romanian): the building blocks every line uses (src/coach/catalog.cpp renders them).
# Same keys as assets/coach/speech/en/common.lang, plus the grammatical forms Romanian needs.
# Register (see the glossary): the coach says "tu" to the player (the game menus use the formal plural);
# the coach is male ("sunt mulțumit"). The player's gender is unknown: no adjective, passive or
# predicative participle agreeing with the player ("ești atent", "ai fost prins", "ai rămas singur");
# the active past is invariable and fine ("ai pierdut", "ai găsit", "te-ai grăbit").
# Orthography: ș ț Ș Ț with the comma below (U+0219 U+021B U+0218 U+021A), never the cedilla; â and î as
# the current norm writes them ("sunt", "câmp", "în"); quotation marks „…”.
# No value of this file may be empty: tools/gen_opening_lang.py reads it and rejects an empty value.

# ---- Pieces --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nouns (the Romanian Chess Federation's words): pionul (m), calul (m), nebunul (m), turnul (n), dama (f),
# regele (m). A neuter noun agrees like a masculine one in the singular, so only the queen is feminine:
# "dama ta", "ea", "o", "legată". piece.<type>.nom is the bare noun ("cal"), said in the spoken moves
# ("cal ef trei", "se transformă în damă"); the placeholders {your} {my} {t1}... never give it.
# A piece placeholder carries its article and its owner: never write "tău", "meu", "alb", an article or
# "al" next to it. The forms, each defined for the six pieces and the four owners (your, my, white, black;
# the forms without an owner word are the same for all four):
#   (nom)     {your} = "calul tău" / "dama ta", {my} = "calul meu" / "dama mea", Analysis {piece} = "calul alb"
#             / "dama albă", "calul negru" / "dama neagră". Subject, direct object, and after every
#             preposition taking the accusative (cu, pe, la, în, din, de, spre, lângă, pentru, fără, sub,
#             până la): the article stays there because the owner word follows. "Ia {my} cu {your}!"
#   gen       "calului tău" / "damei tale", "calului meu" / "damei mele", "calului alb" / "damei albe",
#             "calului negru" / "damei negre": genitive and dative, after a definite noun, after the
#             prepositions taking the genitive (în fața, în spatele, împotriva, deasupra, în jurul) and the
#             dative ones (datorită, mulțumită). "{t2} stă în spatele {t1:gen}.", "Dau șah {your:gen}."
#   def       "calul" / "dama": article, no owner word, for a piece whose owner the sentence already gives
#             (the subject, or a clitic îți / îmi / își). "Mută-ți {your:def} de acolo.", "Îmi retrag {my:def}."
#   def_gen   "calului" / "damei", likewise. "Dă-i șah {my:def_gen}!"
#   bare      "cal" / "damă", no article: a trade or a material count. "Un schimb: {my:bare} pentru {your:bare}."
#   indef     "un cal" / "o damă". "Ai câștigat {my:indef}." After în, din, prin write "într-{my:indef}",
#             "dintr-{my:indef}" ("într-un cal" / "într-o damă"), never "în {my:indef}".
#   indef_gen "unui cal" / "unei dame". "Asta e valoarea {my:indef_gen}."
#   pron      "el" / "ea": after a preposition, or as an emphatic subject (Romanian usually drops it).
#             "{my} atacă {t1}, iar {t2} stă chiar lângă {t1:pron}.", "Totul se sprijină pe {your:pron}."
#   pron_gen  "lui" / "ei", also for "its". "{t2} e în spatele {t1:pron_gen}.", "câmpurile {your:pron_gen}"
#   cl        "îl" / "o", the object clitic before a verb. "Nu {your:cl} mai poți salva.", "{my:cl} poți lua."
#             Not before a perfect or conditional auxiliary: "l-am luat" but "am luat-o" put the clitic in
#             different places. Use the present or the future there, or name the piece again.
#   cl_imp    "-l" / "-o" after an imperative that does not end in -ă. "Ia{my:cl_imp}!" = "Ia-l!" / "Ia-o!"
#   cl_imp_a  "ă-l" / "-o" after the stem of an imperative in -ă (the ă drops before -o): write the verb
#             without its ă. "Mut{your:cl_imp_a} de acolo!" = "Mută-l" / "Mut-o", "Apăr{your:cl_imp_a}!"
#             The short "dă", "fă" keep their ă ("dă-l" / "dă-o"): they take cl_imp.
#   cl_ger    "u-l" / "-o" after a gerund. "Câștigi timp atacând{my:cl_ger}." = "atacându-l" / "atacând-o"
#   dem       "acesta" / "aceasta". "{your:dem} este {your}." = "Acesta este calul tău." / "Aceasta este dama ta."
#   cel       "cel" / "cea". "{your} e {your:cel} mai activ{your:agr_v}.", "{t1:cel} de pe {sq}"
#   ul        "ul" / "a", the article of an adjective standing for the piece, written joined to it.
#             "{your} e singur{your:ul} care apără {sq}." = "singurul" / "singura"; "prim{t1:ul}", "ultim{t1:ul}"
#   rel_gen   "căruia" / "căreia". "{your}, {your:rel_gen} nu-i mai rămâne niciun câmp, e pierdu{your:agr_t}."
#   agr_t agr_s agr_v agr_r agr_l agr_c agr_n agr_b
#             the last letter of an adjective or participle agreeing with the piece, masculine "t" /
#             feminine "tă" (s/să, v/vă, r/ră, l/lă, c/că, n/nă, b/bă): write the word without its last
#             letter. "{your} este lega{your:agr_t}." = "legat" / "legată"; "prin{t1:agr_s}" (prins),
#             "pasi{t1:agr_v}" (pasiv), "libe{t1:agr_r}" (liber), "vulnerabi{t1:agr_l}", "puterni{t1:agr_c}",
#             "bu{t1:agr_n}", "sla{t1:agr_b}". Only for words whose feminine just adds -ă: "mort / moartă",
#             "periculos / periculoasă" do not, rephrase them.
# Two pieces ({t1} și {t2}) cannot share one agreeing word: agree with "piesele" ("ambele piese sunt
# atacate") or use a verb ("atac și {t1}, și {t2}").
# The Analysis mode names a piece by its colour, "calul alb", "dama neagră" (the board shows which piece is
# meant, so "nebunul alb" is White's bishop, not the light-squared one).
